{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791296777,"nonce":"Mz8oz2F7Z0tfDUIrUw73K0CwxnM=","title":"Bound speculative baseline compilation to direct callees of demanded functions","message":"The first-call compiler queues each direct callee before compiling the requested body. A callee compiled by this speculative queue does the same, recursively walking cold branches and compiling a large part of the library call graph. The jobs carry no call count and cannot expire as stale. A tokenize trace compiles foreign/file/metatable support it never executes. This competes with actual execution and LLVM promotion and retains unused native code.\n\nLimit prefetch to one level: actual native calls and interpreted threshold requests may prepare direct callees; an uncalled speculative compile cannot prefetch its descendants. Actual calls still compile missing targets through their stubs. A regression test runs the real worker, waits for the direct speculative callee and queue quiescence, proves its uncalled descendant remains uncompiled, then executes the branch and verifies its result and native entry.","files":null}]}